  <h2 id="money">Cost, the 10% rule and the IPM example</h2>

  <p>
    A degree should not eat the whole education budget. As a strict planning rule, treat about 10% of your total education budget as the starting cap for college fees.
  </p>

  <p>
    Take IPM. The fee reported for a full IPM programme is about Rs 38.3 lakh. At 10%, that fits a total education budget of Rs 3.83 crore. At the 20% caution line, it fits Rs 1.915 crore.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">The arithmetic</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>38,30,000 / 0.10 = Rs 3,83,00,000 budget at the 10% starting rule.</li>
      <li>38,30,000 / 0.20 = Rs 1,91,50,000 budget at the 20% caution line.</li>
      <li>If your family's whole education budget is a fraction of that, IPM needs a clear reason and a way to pay that avoids a loan.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      These are planning rules, not laws. Spending more can make sense for a licence or a scarce, verified advantage, but you should be able to name it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The other 90% is not spare change. It pays for living costs, a laptop or drawing tools, entrance-exam fees, a medical, current online learning, projects, and the upskilling you will need after the degree.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many of the strongest doors here cost far less. Fees differ a lot inside every field here, and public and private routes are not priced alike. Compare the fee sheet of the specific college, not the brand.
  </p>

  <p>
    Design and data can be learned partly through free or low-cost material such as YouTube courses, official documentation and open courseware. That does not replace an accredited degree, a licence or a medical, so keep those where they are required: B.Arch, CPL, deck officer and law.
  </p>

